---

<Lecture
  chapterSlug="04-functions"
  lessonSlug="02-multi-args"
  hook="Two functions. One calls the other. Smallest example of composition (Think Python §3.4) — and the DRY principle (§3.11)."
>
  <h2>The two functions</h2>
  <CodePlayground
    autoRun
    code={`def sum_three(a, b, c):
    return a + b + c

def average_three(a, b, c):
    return sum_three(a, b, c) / 3   # COMPOSITION — reuse, don't duplicate

print(sum_three(1, 2, 3))      # 6
print(average_three(1, 2, 3))  # 2.0
print(average_three(10, 20, 30))  # 20.0`}
  />

  <Callout tone="pitfall" title="The 'duplicates the addition' version">
    <p>
      A "works but worse" version computes <code>a + b + c</code> in both
      functions. The autograder's <code>test_average_three_uses_sum_three</code>
      heuristic rejects this — you have to actually call <code>sum_three</code>
      from inside <code>average_three</code>.
    </p>
  </Callout>

  <h2>Why composition matters</h2>
  <p>
    From Think Python §3.11: <em>"Functions can make a program smaller by
    eliminating repetitive code. Later, if you make a change, you only have
    to make it in one place."</em>
  </p>

  <h2>Float vs int</h2>
  <CodePlayground
    code={`# / is true division — always returns float
print(6 / 3, type(6 / 3))    # 2.0 <class 'float'>
# // is floor division — returns int when both operands are int
print(6 // 3, type(6 // 3))  # 2 <class 'int'>`}
  />
</Lecture>
